.SHORT TERM...
Issued at 1226 PM PDT Sun Oct 25 2026
(This evening through Monday night)

High cloud cover is streaming in from the west/northwest in
advance of an approaching frontal system (largely expected to
remain north of the region). As such, temperatures this afternoon
will peak in the mid to upper-60s near the coast and low to
mid-70s for inland areas away from the immediate coast and the
interior.

Tonight and into Monday morning, expecting the aforementioned
frontal boundary to push inland over northern California brining a
slight chance for very light rain to the northern offshore waters
and the Sonoma County coastline. Temperatures are forecast to
cool into the lower 40s across the North Bay Valleys and far
southern portions of the Salinas Valley Monday morning with the
rest of the region expecting upper 40s to low 50s.

Temperatures cool some 5-10 degrees on Monday as a cooler air
mass spreads southward across the region in response to the
mid/upper level low pushing into the Pacific Northwest.

&&

.LONG TERM...
Issued at 1226 PM PDT Sun Oct 25 2026
(Tuesday through next Saturday)

Below normal temperatures are forecast to persist through midweek
as the region with zonal flow to troughing persisting over the
West Coast. However, dry conditions are forecast to prevail
through midweek. It isn`t until Friday and the upcoming weekend
that we see rain chances return to the region as a cold front
moves through Friday and into Saturday. There is low to moderate
confidence of this occurring. However, there is still time for
things to change so don`t cancel your tick-or-treating plans yet!

Again, the most pressing threat this week will be minor to
moderate coastal flooding along bayshore locations of the San
Pablo, San Francisco, and Monterey Bays. The Pacific Coast gets
added to the Coastal Flood advisory beginning Monday as we are
expecting up to 1.9 feet of inundation above ground level. This is
due to thermal expansion of the warm waters generated by El Nino
and astronomical high tides through much of the upcoming work
week.

